<!--
  NG-SIEM custom correlation rules - PUC2 (CYNET) Sub Case 2c.
  Deployed by role ng_siem_rules_2c onto the wazuh-manager of the siemng image
  (host ng-siem, {{ puc2_ng_siem_ip }}) provisioned by the integrations substrate.

  UML coverage:
    100100 -> step 3 : payload lands in a monitored path (file-hash telemetry)
    100101 -> step 4/5: hash matches the CTI-SS watchlist -> MALWARE ALERT
    100102 -> step 6 : blocked outbound C2/exfil attempt (firewall source)
    100103 -> step 6 : correlated targeted-attack confirmation

  Rule 100103 alone triggers the puc2-isolate active response (UML steps 9-10)
  - see ng_siem_rules_2c/tasks/main.yml. 100101 deliberately does NOT: it fires
  on the first payload drop, and containing there would cut the delivery short
  before 100102 ever sees the C2 beacon.

  MITRE ATT&CK: T1566 Phishing, T1204 User Execution,
                T1021 Lateral Movement, T1048 Exfiltration.
-->
<group name="puc2,malware,local,">

  <!-- TEMPORARY CANARY - remove once the question below is answered.

       Rule 100102 has been keyed four ways and analysisd has never once
       mentioned it, neither as a warning nor in a rule traversal. Two
       explanations remain and nothing collected so far separates them: this
       file is not parsed at all, or it is parsed and wazuh-logtest simply does
       not load etc/rules into its session, in which case the probe can never
       pass no matter how correct the rule is.

       This rule is designed to be REJECTED loudly. Wazuh warns when if_sid
       names a signature that does not exist - it does exactly that for the
       built-in rules 80781 and 80784 on every run - and then ignores the rule.
       So it cannot fire, cannot alert and cannot affect the exercise.

       If a warning naming 100199 appears in the ruleset load, this file IS
       parsed and the problem is where 100102 sits in the tree.
       If no such warning appears, this file is not being read by whatever
       loaded that ruleset, and the instrument is what is wrong. -->
  <rule id="100199" level="0">
    <if_sid>999999</if_sid>
    <description>PUC2 canary: must be rejected with a warning naming 100199</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Suspicious payload written to a user-writable path.
       550 = integrity checksum changed, 554 = file added to the system. -->
  <rule id="100100" level="8">
    <if_sid>550,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file">\.(exe|scr|js|vbs|ps1|sh)$</field>
    <description>PUC2: suspicious payload file created (possible phishing payload)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- File hash present on the CTI/IOC watchlist seeded from CTI-SS (MISP).
       syscheck exposes the post-change hash as md5_after. -->
  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <list field="md5_after" lookup="match_key">etc/lists/cti-malware-hashes</list>
    <description>PUC2: file hash matches CTI-SS malware IOC - MALWARE DETECTED</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1566</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Outbound C2 / exfiltration attempt, blocked and logged by the endpoint's
       pre-staged iptables LOG+DROP rule for tcp/{{ puc2_c2_port }}.

       NOTHING BELOW MAY NEST A TAG INSIDE A COMMENT. Wazuh parses this file
       with OS_XML, not a real XML parser, and every earlier version of this
       comment named the rule options it was discussing in angle brackets. The
       rule was rewritten three times - keyed on a custom decoder, then on a
       group, then on this explicit parent - and analysisd produced a byte for
       byte identical result each time, never once mentioning rule 100102 in
       its ruleset warnings or its rule traversal. That is the signature of a
       file that is not being read at all, not of a rule in the wrong place, so
       the nested tags are gone until the file is proven to load.

       Parent is 4100, the built-in "firewall rules grouped" rule that
       wazuh-logtest shows matching the endpoint's kernel line. The built-in
       kernel decoding already populates srcip, dstip, srcport, dstport and
       protocol, so no custom decoder is involved and no port condition is
       needed: the endpoint's LOG rule carries the {{ puc2_fw_log_prefix }}
       prefix on tcp/{{ puc2_c2_port }} and nothing else, so the prefix alone
       identifies a blocked C2 beacon. Evidence for UML step 6 and level L11
       must not hinge on field extraction succeeding. -->
  <rule id="100102" level="10">
    <if_sid>4100</if_sid>
    <match>{{ puc2_fw_log_prefix }}</match>
    <description>PUC2: blocked outbound connection from endpoint (possible C2/exfil)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1048</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Correlated targeted-attack pattern: the IOC is seen repeatedly inside the
       window. inject_scenario.sh performs a multi-stage delivery precisely so
       this rule is reachable - a single payload drop would fire 100101 once and
       leave UML step 6 without a live trigger. -->
  <rule id="100103" level="14" frequency="2" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_sid>100101</if_matched_sid>
    <description>PUC2: correlated targeted malware attack confirmed (repeated IOC + C2)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1021</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
